3 < 1 ## Story planning — TinyApp targets (`stories-v1`)
4 < 1 
5 < 1 A TinyApp plan is not written; it is compiled from a bundle the author drafts
6 < 1 after enriching the ask with the operator, who then signs it in one question. Specs:
7 < 1 `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-28-coarse-input-planning-design.md` and
8 < 1 `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-28-enrich-the-ask-design.md`. Before signing,
10 > 1 the operator sees the whole page as the real screen, picks one of Jev's versions of it,
11 < 1 pins notes on it, and watches each story, its clicks and checks read off that page, play on it; the real app is judged
9 < 2 the operator sees each piece's real screen, picks one of Jev's versions of it,
10 < 2 pins notes on it, and watches each story play on it; the real app is judged
11 < 1 again at the PR smoke.
12 < 1 
13 < 1 1. **Read the notebook first:** `bun <plugin-root>/skills/ultrawrite/stories/notebook.ts show`.
14 < 1    Use its words; avoid its failed ones.
15 < 1 2. **Take the ask as it comes**, in any form. Save it verbatim to `<bundle>/ask.txt`.
16 < 1    The ask file holds only the operator's own words for this plan: never notes, earlier picks or the author's lines, because Jev reads every sentence in it.
17 < 1 3. **Enrich the ask — every plan, never skipped:** follow
18 < 1    `skills/ultrawrite/references/enrich.md` (intent, understanding, the whole
19 < 1    product's map, the build order) into `<bundle>/product.json`. Draft no story
20 < 1    until the operator has confirmed which plan comes first. A small, clear ask
21 < 1    passes through quickly; it still gets the understanding and a one-line map.
22 < 1 4. **Draft the bundle for that plan**, starting from `skills/ultrawrite/catalog/<piece>/`
23 < 1    wherever one fits:
24 < 1    - `page.json`: its title, kind, three summary sentences, `subproject`, links as
25 < 1      sentences (run inside the trigger's `store.transaction`), numbers, and stories,
27 > 1      each a sentence with its `steps`. A step is `{tool, args, layer}` (`store`, `ui`
28 < 1      or `saved`); leave out a `ui` step's on-screen half (`ui` and `see`): step 7
29 < 1      reads it off the picked page. A step's `"as": "<email>"` (or `null`) sets who
30 < 1      is signed in from that step on.
26 < 2      each with its `steps`. A step is `{tool, args, layer}` (`store`, `ui` or
27 < 2      `saved`); a `ui` step adds `ui` (click/type/key by role and name) and `see`.
28 < 2      The accessible names you choose are the builder's contract. A step's
29 < 2      `"as": "<email>"` (or `null`) sets who is signed in from that step on.
30 < 1    - To deploy when the run lands, `page.json` carries `publish`: `{"deploy": "bun install && bun run deploy", "verify": "curl -fsS \"$ULTRA_PUBLISH_URL/health\""}` (`server/wrangler.jsonc` names the Worker; the account is a `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=` prefix in the app's `deploy` script, since celld refuses `account_id` in the config; see `references/greenfield-stack.md`).
31 < 1    - `cards.json`, each card with its `concept`, or several with `concepts`: one per
33 > 1      map line its screen covers. Each action carries its `label` (the button's
34 < 1      words) and each input its `x-label` (the text box's name); the page is
36 > 1      arranged from these. An action whose inputs take rows of two tables (two
37 < 1      `x-row-of`, as Potluck's `bringDish(dishId, guestId)`) is drawn as a picker
38 < 1      in the list of the first input's table, choosing a row of the second. Put
39 < 1      `x-where` on the picked input (`"x-where": {"answer": "yes"}`) to narrow
40 < 1      what the picker offers to the rows whose cells match. A refusal the picker
41 < 1      makes impossible (it rejects a row the picker never offers) gets no story:
42 < 1      waive it in `page.json` `waivers` with `"reason": "unreachable-from-screen"`
43 < 1      and `arg` the picked input (`guestId`).
44 < 1      Give each card the lines its piece shows, as `shows`: a row line
45 < 1      `{"text": "${name} · ${answer}", "in": "guests"}` is drawn on each row of a
46 < 1      table the card holds, its slots that table's columns; a page line `{"text": "..."}`
47 < 1      carries values in the card's `state.values` or totals over a table some card
48 < 1      holds: `${sum dishes.serves}`, `${count guests}`, `${count guests answer=yes}`,
49 < 1      as Potluck's dish card's `{"text": "Food for ${sum dishes.serves} · ${count guests answer=yes} coming"}`.
50 < 1      One card's `{"heading": "${title}"}` becomes the page heading; only one card
51 < 1      gives a heading. Give the reader's words for a stored value as `words`, slot
52 < 1      to stored value to words: the guest card's `"words": {"answer": {"yes": "coming", "no": "not coming"}}`.
53 < 1      The checks refuse a line naming a cell or value no card holds, or in a table
54 < 1      its card does not hold.
35 < 2      arranged from these.
32 < 2      map line its screen covers.
33 < 1    - `store.js` exports `TOOLS` and `makeStore`.
34 < 1 5. **Write the stories the coverage rule requires**, not ones you invent:
35 < 1    - every card's main story
36 < 1    - one story per refusal, ending in a `ui` step with `"refused": "<the refusal sentence>"`
37 < 1    - one story per link
38 < 1 
39 < 1    A step's `see` names only text its own piece, or a piece it depends on, draws:
40 < 1    on potluck run-1 (2026-10-01) S3's `see` read the answer piece's text from a
41 < 1    guest step, and the guest builder drew it too. A link story's check goes to the
42 < 1    piece of its link, the tool's owner included, that depends on every other one
43 < 1    (#1490, #1525), so it may read the text of any piece in the link; with no such
44 < 1    piece it goes to the tool's owner and reads only that piece's text.
45 < 1 
46 < 1    The only alternative to a refusal story is a waiver
47 < 1    `{piece, action, refuses, arg, "reason": "unreachable-from-screen"}`, where `arg`
48 < 1    is a row id the screen can't invent. The operator never sees waivers; they
49 < 1    appear on the PR card.
